er","narrativeScore":0.012,"accessibilityScore":0.003,"scholarlyScore":0.356,"traits":[{"id":"academic","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Oxford University Press / Oxford University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Oxford University Press / Oxford University Press"]},{"id":"reported","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"embedded\""]},{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]},{"id":"accessible","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\""]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["intellectual history topic"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Fischer argues that liberty and freedom in America are folkways—popular beliefs embedded in culture—whose meanings shift through time rather than serving as fixed philosophical or political abstractions.","confidence":0.78},"academicOrientation":{"score":65,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.8,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e78b1a5ab80ea4c82f0dec7b12e58c33413d56c1fa26aba0402b8ac76efdf5f9","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"American History","secondarySubjects":["Politics & Government","Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book explores the concepts of liberty and freedom as deeply embedded in American culture and history, making its primary focus American History, while also addressing political and societal implications.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"7cc22744cc968fedef6624faf5702eab62301b48a54018f454838d71de85ef75","summary":"A distinguished history and author of Washington's Crossing analyzes the concepts of liberty and freedom through visions, images, and symbols throughout the folk history of those ideas, showing how they are popular beliefs deeply embedded in American culture rather than political abstractions. Liberty and freedom: Americans agree that these values are fundamental to our nation, but what do they mean? How have their meanings changed through time? In this new volume of cultural history, David Hackett Fischer shows how these varying ideas form an intertwined strand that runs through the core of American life. Fischer examines liberty and freedom not as philosophical or political abstractions, but as folkways and popular beliefs deeply embedded in American culture. Tocqueville called them \"habits of the heart.\" From the earliest colonies, Americans have shared ideals of liberty and freedom, but with very different meanings. Like DNA these ideas have transformed and recombined in each generation. The book arose from Fischer's discovery that the words themselves had differing origins: the Latinate \"liberty\" implied separation and independence. The root meaning of \"freedom\" (akin to \"friend\") connoted attachment: the rights of belonging in a community of freepeople.","publicationYear":2005,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Politics and government","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-liberty-and-freedom-a-visual-history-of-america-s-founding-ideas-david-hackett-fischer"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"American National characteristics","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-liberty-and-freedom-a-visual-history-of-america-s-founding-ideas-david-hackett-fischer"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Liberty","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-liberty-and-freedom-a-visual-history-of-america-s-founding-ideas-david-hackett-fischer"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Pictori